Ch. 6 — Decorators and Closures
Formal spine. A closure is a function plus the bindings of its enclosing scope’s free variables. A decorator is a callable D such that
\text{@D} \atop \text{def f(x): ...} \quad\equiv\quad \text{f = D(f)}
— pure syntactic sugar. The decorator is invoked at function-definition time (i.e., at module import) and replaces f with whatever it returns.
Closures via cell objects. Python implements free-variable capture via cell objects — a level of indirection between the inner function and the enclosing local. A function’s __closure__ attribute is a tuple of cells; each cell’s cell_contents is the captured value. The nonlocal keyword (Python 3.0+) is required when the inner function rebinds a captured name (without it, the rebinding creates a local instead).
The well-behaved decorator.
def my_decorator(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        # before
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        # after
        return result
    return wrapper

Two non-negotiable practices:

@functools.wraps(func) copies __name__, __doc__, __qualname__, __module__, __wrapped__ from the original to the wrapper — without it, decorators destroy introspection.
*args, **kwargs accepts the original signature unchanged.

Standard-library decorators.

@functools.cache (3.9+) — memoise calls; arguments must be hashable.
@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=N) — bounded version.
@functools.singledispatch — first-argument-type generic dispatch (poor man’s multimethod).
@property, @classmethod, @staticmethod — built-in descriptors expressed as decorators.

Parameterised decorators. A decorator that takes arguments is a function returning a decorator. Three layers of nesting (decorator-factory → decorator → wrapper) are required; the awkwardness of this is sometimes a sign that a class-based decorator (__init__, __call__) is cleaner.
Cross-link. The functional-programming influence is direct: closures + first-class functions = Lisp’s lambda calculus story imported into a class-based language. Compare to Robert Nystrom - Crafting Interpreters ch. 25 (clox upvalues — the runtime mechanism that makes Python’s __closure__ cells possible at the language level).
